I am not a numbers expert, but I would not encourage that thinking. -10
AC's good at the beginning, but generally "as low as possible" is the
best, and -10 is ultimately a drop in the bucket. You weren't that deep
in the mines, but you were getting deeper, and a lot more stuff could
show up that could have gotten you before Mr. Cockatrice. Maybe not
this game, obviously, but the next.

I recall a quandary I had when as a wizard, I found a blessed scroll of
enchant armor and my only armor was still my Cloak of Magic Resistance.
I was reluctant to use the scroll right away because I thought that
I'll get Magicbane and possibly some other items that grant MR, so the
Cloak's usefulness would ultimately become obsolete. Wouldn't it be
better to save the scroll for something else, I asked? On the other
hand, it was the _only_ armor I had at the moment, and a wizard needs
the best AC they can get, especially since they are effectively
restricted from metal armor.

One of the regs (might have been Janis?) made the sage comment to the
effect of, "Better be alive now to get the better armor later." That
meant, use the darn scroll.

You had not one but several ?ofEA. You could have dropped your AC much
lower--even lowe than a blue dragon's--and therefore not felt as
compelled to play the polymorph game. Mind you, the polymorph game is
fun, I'm sure.

Not to mention, one of your items was gauntlets of power, likely
something you'd want to wear through the whole game, so you should've
at least rustproofed and enchanted up those.

Even if you didn't want to boost the enchantment on everything, at
least fooproofing some of your armor would not have been a bad idea,
especially since you mention trouble with rust monsters.

Just friendly advice--if in doubt, use the ?ofEA

